## Agent behavior rules (bounded-pr-loop)

These rules apply to **all automated work** in this repo. Both Claude and
Codex must follow them. Violations cause the review gate to FAIL.

### 1. Scope discipline

- Implement only what the issue asks for. Do not refactor unrelated code in
  the same PR, even if it is tempting.
- If a change requires touching adjacent code to compile, do it minimally and
  call it out in the PR description.

### 2. Tests

- Add at least one test for new behavior unless the change is purely
  cosmetic (formatting, comments, docs).
- Run the project's test command before pushing. Do not push if tests fail.

### 3. PR hygiene

- PR description must include `Closes #<issue-number>` so the originating
  issue auto-closes on merge.
- Commit messages start with `[agent]` followed by a conventional-commit
  type: `[agent] feat:`, `[agent] fix:`, `[agent] docs:`, etc.
- Keep each commit reviewable in isolation.

### 4. Fix loops

- When fixing a Codex review, address **only** the listed blocking findings.
- Do not add new functionality during a fix loop.
- If you cannot fix a finding without breaking scope rule (1), say so in a
  PR comment and stop — the harness will hand off to a human.

### 5. Sensitive paths (hard stop)

Do not modify these paths in an automated PR. The harness will block the
merge anyway, but flagging early saves a loop iteration:

- `.github/workflows/**`
- `**/auth/**`
- `**/billing/**`
- `**/migrations/**`
- `**/secrets/**`

Anything beyond this list that you suspect is sensitive: stop and ask.

### 6. Secrets

- Never commit API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, or production
  configuration. `gitleaks` runs on every PR and will block the merge if it
  finds something, but you should never write one in the first place.
- Reference secrets via environment variables; assume they are present.

### 7. Documentation

- If a public-facing behavior changes, update this file or the relevant doc
  in the same PR. A PR that changes behavior without updating its docs is
  failing rule (1) — scope is incomplete, not minimal.

